  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1211 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nAnother pleasant day is progressing across the Mid-South with dry\nconditions and temperatures spanning the 80s. With the persistent, \ndry conditions, high temperatures will once again warm into the \n90s for much of the area by this afternoon. While conditions will \nnot feel as oppressive as they have earlier in the month, this \nwill close out an unseasonably warm and likely record-breaking \nSeptember across the Mid-South. As climate records stand\ncurrently, Memphis, Jackson, and Jonesboro will likely all break\ntheir average temperature records for the month of September, \nwith Tupelo right on the edge of tying or exceeding their record. \nClimate records will be finalized after today; however, September \n2026 will certainly go down in the record books in some capacity. \nDry conditions will persist through Thursday with high pressure \nremaining in place to our east and high temperatures once again \nexpected to reach the low 90s.\n\nBy Friday, a notable pattern shift will arrive across the Mid-\nSouth as a low-pressure system currently bringing heavy rainfall\nwest of the region shifts across the Southeast. Guidance around\nthis system has continued to struggle, both on the timing of the\nfrontal boundary descending from the northwest as well as how\nmuch precipitation the Mid-South will see as a result. As things\nstand currently, this does not appear to be an entire washout for\nthe Mid-South, but a healthy, much-needed rain event. The initial\nfront will arrive on Friday, increasing shower and thunderstorm\nchances through the day with PWAT values surging to 1.75 to 2 \ninches across the area. This front will stall generally across or \njust to the south of the area, keeping chances in the forecast \nthrough Saturday into early Sunday. The boundary will finally be \nushered out of the area Sunday into early Monday. Currently, LREF \nprobabilities have about a 40-50% chance of exceeding 1 inch of \nrainfall along and south of the I-40 corridor, with less than a \n20% chance of exceeding 2 inches through Sunday evening. These \nprobabilities are low considering deterministic output is painting\nup to 2 inches across northeast Mississippi, although latest runs\nhave been showing a downward trend. While an inch of rainfall is \nwelcome considering many did not even receive that much rainfall \nfor the entire month of September, this does not appear to be a \ndrought-busting rainfall event. From a severe weather standpoint, \nthere does not appear to be much in the way of upper-level \nsupport, with guidance showing a modest, skinny CAPE profile on \nsoundings. While convection could increase our rainfall totals, \nsevere weather appears to be unlikely.\n\nBy Monday, a mostly dry and seasonable forecast emerges across\nthe area as a weak upper-level ridge develops by midweek. Current\nforecast trends favor daily high temperatures spanning the 70s,\nwith overnight lows in the 50s. These values would be around to\njust below normal for the beginning of October, which is certainly \nwelcome given the anomalously warm September we have recently \nexperienced.",
